I try to remember that incident whenever I’m tempted to be selfish with my things or start to[ prize] them too highly. It’s great to have nice things and[ gro]ovy[ gadgets]. (Don’t tell anyone, but I’m fairly addicted to my phone, which contains all my appointments, mail, reminders, to-do lists, Facebook, Twitter,[ Messenger], Google, and favorite games.) These days, the “must-have”[ gadgets] are[ endless]—from smartphones, iPads, laptops, and gaming equipment right down to 3D home TVs! If you’re able to have some of these nice things and enjoy them and they help you with your work and studies, or are also a fun source of entertainment, technology can be great. But it’s good to step back sometimes and imagine what it would be like if one day all those things instantly disappeared. After your initial withdrawal symptoms, you’d realize that life goes on without[ material] things. What matters most are our loved ones—our friends and family members. Sometimes we can forget this truth, and that’s when we tend to act selfishly

